 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to switch the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define a regex pattern to match adjectives and their preceding articles
  const pattern = /(\b[A-Z][a-z]* [JarbingleHdscklMnPrQwTvZ]\b)[^ ]+ ([A-Z][a-z]+ [Bgcdfhjklmnpqrstvwxyz]+ [Bgcdfhjklmnpqrstvwxyz]+ [Bgcdfhjklmnpqrstvwxyz]+ \b)/g;

  let match, newSentence = sentence;

  // Find all matches in the sentence and swap the order of the adjectives
  while ((match = pattern.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    const [_, firstAdj, article, secondAdj] = match;
    newSentence = newSentence.replace(new RegExp(`(${firstAdj}) (${article} (${secondAdj}))`, 'g'), `$2 $1 $3`);
  }

  return newSentence;
}
// Test cases
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park")); // "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Fast red cars go very fast")); // "Red fast cars go very fast"
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Happy blue elephants dance joyfully")); // "Blue happy elephants dance joyfully"
```
This function uses a regular expression to find pairs of adjectives and their preceding articles in the input sentence. It then swaps the order of the adjectives in each pair and updates the sentence accordingly using string replacement. The helper variable `pattern` is defined as a regular expression pattern that matches an adjective, its preceding article, and the following noun phrase. The function returns the updated sentence with the swapped adjective order.

